.TH FLEET-JOURNAL 1 "2026-11-03" "cti-agent 1.4" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
fleet-journal \- read the fleet's own journal units, and nothing else journalctl can do
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fleet\-journal
.BR \-u " " \fIcti\-agent\-UNIT\fR
.RB [ \-u
.IR cti\-agent\-UNIT ...]
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR WHEN ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-pager ]
.br
.B fleet\-journal \-h
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent fleet\-journal
.I options
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fleet\-journal
is the orchestrator heartbeat's only way into the journal: it shows what the
fleet's timers did and why one failed. It is a restricted front end to
.BR journalctl (1)
and is installed root-owned in
.IR $FLEET_CODE/bin ,
where
.BR run\-checkin (1)
checks it with the rest of the code.
.PP
The heartbeat used to be granted
.B journalctl
with any arguments.
.B journalctl
is not read-only:
.B \-\-cursor\-file=PATH
writes the last cursor to
.I PATH
as the calling account. A beat steered by a hostile advisory could aim that at
.I state/budget.json
(resetting the quota that keeps the fleet from crowding out its owner) or
.IR state/memory.db ,
and every later beat would run on the damage;
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-\-directory ,
.B \-\-merge
and others widen what it can read. The wrapper accepts only the options below,
admits only the fleet's own units, and passes each value to
.B journalctl
as
.BI \-\-option= VALUE
so that nothing it is given can be read as a further option. It always adds
.BR \-\-no\-pager .
.PP
Anything else is refused before
.B journalctl
runs: a usage line goes to standard error and the exit status is 2. The
operator, who has a shell, uses
.B journalctl
directly.
.SH OPTIONS
At least one
.B \-u
is required.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unit " \fIUNIT\fR"
Show this unit. Repeatable. The name must begin with
.B cti\-agent\-
and contain only letters, digits and
.BR @ ,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.B \-
and
.BR * ;
globs are allowed, so
.B \-u 'cti\-agent\-*'
reads every lane. Any other unit is refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " WHEN"
Only entries since
.IR WHEN ,
in a form
.B journalctl
accepts:
.BR today ,
.BR yesterday ,
.BR \-3h ,
.BR "2026\-11\-03 06:00:00" .
Letters, digits, spaces and
.B : . + \-
only, at most 40 characters.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-lines " \fIN\fR"
Only the last
.I N
entries. Digits only, at most six.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pager
Accepted and ignored: output is never paged.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage line to standard error and exit 2.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B journalctl
ran and succeeded (it replaces the wrapper, so its status is the
wrapper's).
.TP
.B 2
Refused: an option not listed above, a missing value, a unit outside
.BR cti\-agent\-* ,
a malformed
.B \-\-since
or
.BR \-n ,
no
.B \-u
at all, or
.BR \-h .
.TP
.I other
.BR journalctl 's
own exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
What the digest lane did today:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-journal \-u cti\-agent\-digest.service \-\-since today
.EE
.PP
Every lane, last two hours:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-journal \-u 'cti\-agent\-*' \-\-since \-2h \-n 200
.EE
.PP
A refused request, as the orchestrator would see it:
.PP
.EX
$ fleet\-journal \-u cti\-agent\-digest \-\-cursor\-file=/tmp/x
fleet\-journal: '\-\-cursor\-file=/tmp/x' is not accepted (only \-u, \-\-since, \-n and \-\-no\-pager)
$ echo $?
2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
fleet\-kit/fleet/CLAUDE.md; SECURITY.md, "What the orchestrator may run".
